      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here is a concise summary of the appellate court judgment, hitting the core legal principles and the final holding, while avoiding unnecessary citations:\n\nThe UK Supreme Court held that the duty of care owed by a vessel operator to third-party contractors is contingent upon the foreseeability of harm within the specific operational context of the maritime corridor. This liability is not absolute but subject to the proximity requirement, similar to Donoghue v Stevenson ([1932] AC 562). The court rejected the appellant's argument that the expansion of negligence principles in Caparo Industries plc v Dickman ([1990] 2 AC 605) should be applied more restrictively, due to the specifics of the Merchant Shipping Act 1995. The court's holding reflects a more nuanced interpretation of the duty of care in maritime liability cases.",
